    "['ip ospf [process_id] area [area_id]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set protocols ospf area 0 interface GigabitEthernet1/0/0', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"##\\nset protocols ospf area 0 interface GigabitEthernet1/0/0\\nset protocols ospf area 0 interface GigabitEthernet2/0/0\\nset protocols ospf area 0 interface Loopback1\\n##\\n\\nAlternatively, for a more concise format (Juniper often uses wildcards):\\n\\n##\\nset protocols ospf area 0 interface all\\n##\\n\\nNote: The Juniper equivalent doesn't require the OSPF process ID (1) since Juniper doesn't use process IDs in the same way as Cisco. The `passive-interface` equivalent would be a separate command in Juniper.\", 'trans_template': 'set protocols ospf area 0 interface GigabitEthernet1/0/0'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set protocols ospf area 0 interface GigabitEthernet2/0/0', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"##\\nset protocols ospf area 0 interface GigabitEthernet1/0/0\\nset protocols ospf area 0 interface GigabitEthernet2/0/0\\nset protocols ospf area 0 interface Loopback1\\n##\\n\\nAlternatively, for a more concise format (Juniper often uses wildcards):\\n\\n##\\nset protocols ospf area 0 interface all\\n##\\n\\nNote: The Juniper equivalent doesn't require the OSPF process ID (1) since Juniper doesn't use process IDs in the same way as Cisco. The `passive-interface` equivalent would be a separate command in Juniper.\", 'trans_template': 'set protocols ospf area 0 interface GigabitEthernet2/0/0'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set protocols ospf area 0 interface Loopback1', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"##\\nset protocols ospf area 0 interface GigabitEthernet1/0/0\\nset protocols ospf area 0 interfac